  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nOur next prolonged dry stretch is underway as a 1034 hPa \nanticyclone continues to inch farther eastward from the heart of\nthe Ohio Valley beneath a large- scale ridge aloft. Fair weather\nthroughout the remainder of the week and into the weekend will\nbe met with daily moderations in temperatures as upper-level\nheights continue to build about the ridge and the surface high,\nthough remaining to our south, nears. In fact, though highs \nwill be a few degrees warmer than yesterday, today will be the\ncoolest day of the next many with values reaching the mid 40s to\nmid 50s. The remainder of the week and into the weekend, highs\nwill largely be in the upper 40s to upper 50s though Friday, the\nwarmest of the next seven days, will largely feature low 50s to\nlow 60s. \n\nA weak shortwave trough still looks to graze our northern areas\non Thursday night into Friday, though the previously mentioned \nback-door cold front seems to now become washed out to our north\nbefore really making a passage through our area. Still, no \nprecipitation was expected with this moisture-starved \ndisturbance. In fact, the next chance for precipitation doesn't \nlook to come until Monday with the passage of a frontal system.",
